WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.849 --> 00:00:05.740
The new Dune movie is a spectacular cinematic
experience that's vastly different to David

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.559
Lynch's vividly weird take on the original
story by Frank Herbert.

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:14.340
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm explaining the spiciest differences

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.730
between Denis Villeneuve's Dune and the 80s
cult movie.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.560
Lynch and Villeneuve's films are worlds apart
when it comes to the villainous characters

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.210
of Baron Vladimir Harkonnen and his nephew
Rabban aka The Beast.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:32.469
Once you've seen the 1984 version played with
maniacally OTT gusto by Kenneth McMillan,

00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:36.719
who's covered in grotesque prosthetics makeup
and zooms through the air, it's not something

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:38.550
you're ever likely to forget.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:43.890
It is I, Baron Vladimir Harkonnen, who encompasses
his doom!

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.319
Stellan Skarsgard gives a more subtle, less
caricatured take on the character, making

00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:53.469
him memorable more for his quietly menacing
manner than his gruesome cartoonish ways like

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:54.889
the original film.

00:00:54.889 --> 00:00:59.769
Even how he rises into the air feels more
ominous than the exaggerated zoomy way it

00:00:59.769 --> 00:01:01.049
happens in Lynch's movie.

00:01:01.049 --> 00:01:05.700
For Villeneuve, Skarsgard's eyes are the key
to his frightening performance as you feel

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.510
the Baron's always thinking, tense, and calculating
deep inside.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:14.750
The first time we see the Baron, he's hidden
by steam, giving the scene a really atmospheric

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.460
feel, and reminding me of the shadowy scenes
of Marlon Brando's Colonel Kurtz in Apocalypse

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.549
Now as well as the helmet-less Darth Vader.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:28.039
Dave Bautista is back working with Villeneuve
again after Blade Runner 2049, and thankfully

00:01:28.039 --> 00:01:33.020
his interpretation of the intimidating Rabban
is much less theatrical, and sweaty, than

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.020
the 80s film.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:39.110
So far, Rabban and his uncle play a relatively
small part in Villeneuve's cinematic vision

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.390
of Herbert's source material – more on that
later.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:46.549
As for the Baron's younger nephew Feyd, there's
no sign of him at all at the moment.

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:49.500
I do wonder if he'll pop up in Part 2 though.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.689
It'd certainly be interesting to see who Villeneuve
would cast compared to the mad-eyed over-acting

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:56.979
of Sting in Lynch's film!

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:01.690
Despite Dune's dangerous, dog-eat-dog world,
there's one creature that seems pretty much

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.920
safe at all times, and that's the House Atreides
pug!

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.610
Whether sitting patiently at home, travelling
through space, or venturing into battle, the

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:14.940
cute royal canine always appears pretty chill
even if his presence in the film seems a bizarre

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:20.420
choice for a grand space opera aiming to be
"Star Wars for grown-ups", as Princess Irulan

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:22.390
actress Virginia Madsen called it.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:27.360
Though we may never know exactly why Lynch
made this weirdly memorable and often beloved

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.550
addition to the world of Dune, it may have
something to do with this particular breed's

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:36.420
long association with emperors, royalty, and
the upper echelons of society from Ancient

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.290
China to Renaissance Europe and beyond.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.900
Understandably, though sadly for some fans,
there are no pooches present in Villeneuve's

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:49.120
version, however, the director has made his
own strange new addition with a creepy giant

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.480
spider creature with uncanny humanoid features
such as hands at the end of its legs.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.680
The Harkonnen pet is listed in the movie's
credits as a "Human Spider Proxy", and the

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.640
creature's performer is Milena Sidorova, a
choreographer who came up with the famous

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.150
spider dance you might have seen on YouTube.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.770
The spider-creature appears to understand
human communication as it responds to the

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.570
Bene Gesserit Reverend Mother when she orders
it to leave the room.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:18.540
And this has led many fans to speculate that
the Harkonnens turned Doctor's Yueh's wife

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.040
into the spider.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.900
It's a plausible theory given in the original
source material her fate was never known,

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:30.050
although – spoiler warning now for character
fates in Dune 2021 going forward – the Baron

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:33.590
implies Yueh's wife was dead at the time he
kills the doctor.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.150
I said I'd set her free.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:36.690
That you could join her.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:37.690
So join her.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:42.570
The character of Duncan Idaho played by Jason
Momoa is hugely improved in Villeneuve's version

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:46.820
getting more screentime and his relationship
with Paul is better developed than in Lynch's

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:50.180
movie where the two characters share just
a couple of scenes.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:55.820
Villeneuve has described his Duncan as a kind
of "big brother" character who Paul idolizes.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:00.570
Momoa's Duncan also gets an epic fight scene
where he sacrifices himself to take down a

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:04.640
slew of Sardaukar warriors to buy time for
Paul, Jessica and Kynes to escape.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.170
And it's a pretty badass moment when he pulls
a sword out of his chest and takes out another

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:13.840
bunch of baddies before eventually keeling
over.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.720
Duncan's death in Lynch's movie is much more
anti-climactic with a projectile slowly penetrating

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.470
his defensive shield.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:25.930
And those Holtzman energy shields were one
of the oddest things in the 80s movie with

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:30.810
the Roblox-like polygon shapes surrounding
a character making scenes such as Gurney and

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:35.850
Paul's fight training just a mass of blurry
forms facing off, then piling on top of each

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:36.850
other.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.430
Thankfully special effects have massively
improved since then and the personal shields

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:45.400
in 2021's movie are much smoother and less
blurry so you can still see the characters

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.680
clearly enough.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:51.650
Kyle MacLachlan didn't do a bad job as Paul
given the material he had to work with, especially

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:55.990
all those endless internal monologues he and
several of the other actors were forced to

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:56.990
bring to life.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.850
However, Timothée Chalomet's Paul and his
path to becoming the super being known as

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:03.690
the Kwisatz Haderach is much better developed.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:08.570
First of all, although Chalomet was in his
early 20s when filming, Paul is around 15

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:14.190
to 16 in the original book and the actor plays
the part of a somewhat inexperienced teenager

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:19.990
thrust into a deadly world of political manoeuvrings
much more convincingly than in Lynch's movie.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:24.410
Considering that it's originally supposed
to be a coming-of-age story, Lynch's Paul

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:29.920
feels much more like the man in his 20s that
the actor was at the time and too self-assured.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:34.730
Also the way that Villeneuve foreshadows Paul's
connection to Arrakis is teased more elegantly.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:39.610
For starters, Paul's visions of Dune and his
future there are clearer, more nuanced and

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:43.090
detailed than Lynch's more cryptic, clunky
visions.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:48.180
Also, Villeneuve has added more depth to scenes
such as the sandworm attack on the crawler.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.730
In the new movie, as Paul gets out of the
ornithopter, he becomes entranced by the desert

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:55.690
while hearing mysterious voices chanting.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:06:00.850
In Lynch's film, Paul never gets off the ship
and instead simply gets a little intoxicated

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:03.850
by the smell of the spice the men bring in
with them.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:04.850
Spice.

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:07.220
Pure, unrefined spice.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:12.110
The look of Villeneuve's worms is also quite
different, and the director explained to Empire

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.680
magazine that it took over a year to come
up with

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:18.940
"every little detail that would make such
a beast possible, from the texture of the

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:23.840
skin, to the way the mouth opens, to the system
it uses to eat its food in the sand, and to

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.060
find the perfect shape that looked prehistoric
enough."

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:32.040
Overall, the new sandworms seem even larger
than Lynch's version which was more closely

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.670
based on the original designs by the book's
illustrator with its distinctive three-lobed

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:37.670
mouth.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:43.360
Something we didn't get much of in the 2021
movie was worm riding, with only a small moment

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.340
at the end where Paul sees a Fremen riding
a worm in the distance.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.510
Desert power.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:53.199
In Lynch's film, Paul memorably rides a worm
and the creatures are used as part of the

00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:56.660
Fremen resistance and attacks on the Harkonnen
occupiers.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:00.890
Because this happens in the latter part of
the story, it's safe to say we can look forward

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:04.740
to Timothée Chalomet riding a giant worm
in the sequel!

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:09.420
While Lynch's film opens with the Emperor's
daughter introducing us to the world, Villeneuve

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:13.080
gives Chani the job of briefly being our guide
to Arrakis.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.130
Given her prominent placing in the movie's
marketing, you'd be forgiven for thinking

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:21.490
Zendaya must have quite a substantial role,
but that's not the case.

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:26.590
In fact, her screen time consists mostly of
brief glimpses in Paul's visions until near

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:31.110
the end of the film where she and Paul finally
meet and she gets a bit of real dialogue;

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.650
indeed, her words are the final ones we hear
in the movie.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:36.139
This is only the beginning.

00:07:36.139 --> 00:07:41.500
Although Zendaya spent only four days filming
for Part 1, in Part 2 her character will have

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:46.000
a much larger, more important place in the
story which I'm looking forward to as she's

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.690
a talented actress.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:52.150
As for Sean Young, though Lynch covers the
whole book, she's still only a very small

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:56.870
part of a much bigger movie and doesn't get
any especially interesting dialogue to craft

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:58.690
a truly memorable part.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:03.770
When it comes to Paul's mother, Lady Jessica,
Villeneuve has built up her role compared

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:08.560
to Francesca Annis's version, giving her more
time training and defending her son.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.360
Rebecca Ferguson is perfectly cast, bringing
the delicate mix of power, vulnerability,

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.340
and mystery that's required for her character
whose role encompasses parent, partner, and

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:20.960
member of the formidable and influential Bene
Gesserit,

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.930
who have the commanding power of The Voice,
which doubtless inspired the Jedi mind tricks

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:27.620
of Star Wars.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:31.680
Another character who gets more screen time
in the new movie is Dr Kynes.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.899
And compared to Max von Sydow in Lynch's movie,
Sharon Duncan-Brewster goes out heroically,

00:08:36.899 --> 00:08:39.610
exacting revenge on the colonisers ravaging
her planet.

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:41.939
I only serve one master.

00:08:41.939 --> 00:08:43.029
His name is Shai-Hulud.

00:08:43.029 --> 00:08:49.509
Sometimes, Lynch's Dune feels more like a
collective hallucination or weird fever dream

00:08:49.509 --> 00:08:52.480
I had rather than a film I actually watched!

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.619
Its frequent wacky visuals and garish aesthetic
certainly give it a very distinctive look

00:08:57.619 --> 00:08:59.319
which does have its fans.

00:08:59.319 --> 00:09:04.889
The latest film's look, however, is wildly
different, more David Lean, specifically Lawrence

00:09:04.889 --> 00:09:10.009
of Arabia, than David Lynch, with touches
of Apocalypse Now, Villeneuve's own Arrival,

00:09:10.009 --> 00:09:12.699
and cinematographer Greig Fraser's Rogue One.

00:09:12.699 --> 00:09:17.279
There's plenty of lavish, sweeping vistas,
and arresting visuals, but the director never

00:09:17.279 --> 00:09:20.699
loses a sense of what really matters: his
characters.

00:09:20.699 --> 00:09:25.529
And the film's muted, more natural-looking
tones make its pops of colour stand out, but

00:09:25.529 --> 00:09:28.129
in a grounded, more realistic way.

00:09:28.129 --> 00:09:33.220
Trying to pack the whole of Frank Herbert's
expansive sci-fi novel into a single film

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:35.800
did not work out well for David Lynch.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:41.279
The movie ended up heavy on melodrama, and
overloaded with plot and masses of expositionary

00:09:41.279 --> 00:09:42.279
dialogue.

00:09:42.279 --> 00:09:46.399
Not only did it perform poorly at the box
office, but it was also critically mauled,

00:09:46.399 --> 00:09:51.509
with reviewers calling it "incomprehensible",
"stupefyingly dull and disorderly", and the

00:09:51.509 --> 00:09:53.320
"worst movie of the year".

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:57.730
Even Lynch himself disowned the film and viewed
it as a failure, citing his lack of artistic

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.319
freedom making it.

00:09:59.319 --> 00:10:03.660
At the opposite end of the spectrum, Denis
Villeneuve says he's never felt such deep

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.610
joy or gratitude watching one of his movies
as he has with Dune.

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:12.110
Villeneuve actually refused to adapt the novel
unless the story was split over more than

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.600
one film as 'the world is too complex'.

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:19.699
So the new movie brings to life roughly half
the book, building its world and introducing

00:10:19.699 --> 00:10:22.180
its concepts, characters, cultures, and planets.

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:26.610
Though this does mean the ending is a bit
less satisfying than you might like, the sequel

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.209
will cover the rest.

00:10:28.209 --> 00:10:33.879
While for Villeneuve, Part 1 is "like an appetizer",
"Part 2 is the main meal", "an insane playground"

00:10:33.879 --> 00:10:37.350
that will "allow him to go berserk and really
create".

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:41.589
And, as it that's not enough, the director
has even said that he'd like to make a third

00:10:41.589 --> 00:10:44.309
film adapting the second book in the Dune
saga, Messiah.

00:10:44.309 --> 00:10:48.930
I love the overall epic feel of Villeneuve's
Dune, but what do you think?

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:51.879
And if you've seen both movies, which do you
prefer?

00:10:51.879 --> 00:10:55.950
Let me know in the comments below, and if
you enjoyed this, then a thumbs-up and share

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:57.779
are hugely appreciated.

00:10:57.779 --> 00:11:01.670
You can tap here to discover all the epic
easter eggs and details you might have missed

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:05.040
in Squid Game or tap right for something else
you're sure to like.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.329
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:11:07.329 --> 00:11:08.529
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

